WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.460
Proposition forty-three changes the vote threshold for certain local taxes.

2
00:00:05.460 --> 00:00:10.580
A special tax pays for a specified purpose, such as a local service. A

3
00:00:10.580 --> 00:00:15.560
general tax supports general government spending. This measure focuses on

4
00:00:15.560 --> 00:00:20.900
special taxes proposed by voters. Government-proposed local special taxes

5
00:00:20.900 --> 00:00:26.120
generally need two-thirds approval. Court decisions allow voter-proposed

6
00:00:26.120 --> 00:00:31.760
special taxes to pass by a simple majority. Proposition forty-three would

7
00:00:31.760 --> 00:00:34.860
require two-thirds for those voter proposals too.

8
00:00:35.367 --> 00:00:39.707
Imagine residents discussing a proposal for local services. Suppose a

9
00:00:39.707 --> 00:00:45.587
hundred people vote on a citizen-proposed special tax, and sixty vote yes.

10
00:00:45.587 --> 00:00:50.327
That is enough for a simple majority. It is short of two-thirds, so the same

11
00:00:50.327 --> 00:00:55.127
proposal would fail under the new requirement. Local services and the tax

12
00:00:55.127 --> 00:00:56.687
burden are both at stake.

13
00:00:57.233 --> 00:01:01.633
Beginning January first, twenty twenty-seven, the higher threshold would

14
00:01:01.633 --> 00:01:07.833
apply to new, increased, or extended local special taxes proposed by voters.

15
00:01:07.833 --> 00:01:12.893
An extension matters because some existing taxes expire unless voters renew

16
00:01:12.893 --> 00:01:17.993
them. The proposition does not set a new tax rate or immediately cancel

17
00:01:17.993 --> 00:01:23.533
every existing tax. It changes the hurdle future proposals must clear.

18
00:01:23.533 --> 00:01:28.233
Voters would still decide whether a particular tax deserves support. The

19
00:01:28.233 --> 00:01:32.853
analyst expects local revenue could be lower than it otherwise would be, but

20
00:01:32.853 --> 00:01:37.493
gives no fixed amount. The effect depends on which future measures receive

21
00:01:37.493 --> 00:01:40.553
majority support but fall short of two-thirds.

22
00:01:41.133 --> 00:01:45.593
A voter might want broad agreement before a dedicated local tax takes

23
00:01:45.593 --> 00:01:51.613
effect, regardless of whether a council or a citizen group proposes it. They

24
00:01:51.613 --> 00:01:55.993
may see one consistent threshold as protection for taxpayers who would have

25
00:01:55.993 --> 00:01:57.653
to pay despite voting no.

26
00:01:58.167 --> 00:02:02.827
You might ask how much power a minority should have. Another voter could

27
00:02:02.827 --> 00:02:07.007
object that more than half the community may support a service but still be

28
00:02:07.007 --> 00:02:13.247
unable to fund it. In the example, forty no votes block sixty yes votes. A

29
00:02:13.247 --> 00:02:17.187
higher hurdle can protect dissenters while making collective action harder.

30
00:02:17.733 --> 00:02:23.173
In the September PPIC survey of likely voters, 43 percent said yes, 51

31
00:02:23.173 --> 00:02:28.573
percent no, and 6 percent were unsure. These are dated survey results, not

32
00:02:28.573 --> 00:02:34.273
votes already cast. A later September Berkeley survey found 31 percent yes,

33
00:02:34.273 --> 00:02:39.593
40 percent no, and 29 percent undecided. Question wording and methods

34
00:02:39.593 --> 00:02:44.113
differ, so comparing polls does not by itself establish a trend.

35
00:02:44.667 --> 00:02:49.787
Yes raises the threshold for these voter-proposed special taxes; no retains

36
00:02:49.787 --> 00:02:54.947
the current majority route. Visit our website for the fuller explanation,

37
00:02:54.947 --> 00:02:59.527
source links, and official voter guide. Compare the tradeoffs, then make

38
00:02:59.527 --> 00:03:01.207
your own informed choice.
